// Steam is water's gas phase, and the missing return leg of the water
// cycle: boiled off by heat (water.ron's boiling_point), it rises, cools
// through its own conductivity, and condenses back into water at
// cooling_point — so nothing accumulates against a ceiling forever, and
// boiled water comes back instead of only ever leaving the world.
//
// **`aux` on a steam cell carries the source water's fill**, on the same
// convention a Liquid uses (0 means full — see material.rs). fire.rs's
// `transform` copies it across in both directions, which is what makes the
// boil → condense loop conserve water per cell exactly. A gas's aux is
// otherwise unused; nothing else reads or writes it.
//
// Born at the boiling water's own temperature (≥ 100°C), which is what
// keeps the loop alive with no scheduler: a hot cell re-dirties its own
// chunk every frame (fire.rs's must_stay_dirty), so steam can never be
// stranded on a sleeping ceiling — it keeps being visited and keeps
// cooling until condensation fires. cooling_point 45 sits comfortably
// above the thermal settle band (ambient 20 ± 1), so the cooling curve
// always reaches it, sealed pockets included.
(
    name: "steam",
    display: "Steam",
    kind: Gas,
    // Half of smoke's 0.1, so steam rises through smoke over a fire
    // rather than mixing into one grey mass.
    density: 0.05,
    // A little tighter than smoke's 3: steam reads as a plume, not a haze.
    dispersion: 2,
    // Real conductivity, per README's rule: any target of boils_into
    // inherits its source's heat and needs somewhere to put it, or the
    // chunk it lands in never sleeps. 0.12 cools a fresh 100–200°C cell to
    // the 45° condensation threshold in roughly 10–20 visited frames —
    // fast enough that ceilings bead up while the pool still boils, slow
    // enough that the plume is visible on the way there.
    heat_conductivity: 0.12,
    cooling_point: 45.0,
    cools_into: "water",
    // **Tried and reverted: direct-contact condensation** -- a
    // `(with: "water", produces: ("water", "water"))` reaction here, so a
    // steam bubble touching subcooled water collapses into it the way real
    // ones do. Built to drain open-bugs-handoff 0b's trapped delta pocket
    // (~1,400 steam cells at a mean of 163C that `cooling_point` can never
    // reach, because a near-uniform cavity in warm rock has no gradient).
    // It conserves water exactly and it did drain the pocket's steam --
    // and it made the system as a whole *worse*, in both thermal variants,
    // which per CLAUDE.md means the approach and not the tuning: under the
    // exchange rule every collapse handed the bubble's whole heat to one
    // water cell and minted a >=100C boiler (~35 boil/react events a frame,
    // up from ~5.5); under `mixes_heat` both products sat near 100C and
    // the boil/collapse loop churned lossless at ~34 boils a frame with
    // the warm-water population *growing*. Contact condensation moves heat
    // between phases faster than anything here removes it — and the loop
    // never needed a new sink at all: its energy source was 90 cells of
    // hot, thermally-inert rubble acting as permanent radiators inside the
    // delta (see rubble.ron's heat_conductivity note). With that fixed the
    // pocket condenses through the ordinary cooling_point above and the
    // scene sleeps outright.
    colors: [
        (222, 228, 236),
        (206, 214, 226),
        (234, 238, 244),
    ],
)
